  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe use a hybrid evolutionary optimization approach to break the problem into smaller subproblems that can be more efficiently solved. The central idea is to represent the sequence as a set of colored blocks (pulses or peak responses) and evolve their positions, sizes, and weights. This is inspired by the wavelet mathematics, where overcompleteness helps in approximating signals and minimizing autocorrelation peaks. The search space includes sequences with from 3 to 15 distinct blocks (pulses) distributed across the sequence. The evolution will:\n- Randomly initialize blocks with pulse-heights, centers, and widths\n- Use a combinatorial crossover to swap segments between good block configurations\n- Perform pulse height normalization to guarantee positive or zero values\n- Use downscaled optimal algorithms to handle both small and large n sequences\n\nThe blocks are allowed to overlap slightly to capture the maximum problem effectiveness. The objective is to find a sequence where the sum of squared values is minimized relative to the convolution peak, which aligns with the mathematical optimum mentioned in the task documentation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe sequence will be represented as a set of triangular impulse responses (pulses) that we will normalize to ensure the sum is \u22650.01 and each value \u22641000. The mathematical structure:\n\nDefine a novel `pulse_block(t: float) = max(0, 1000.0 - k * min_amplification)` but we are using discrete pulses to approximate.\n\nThe objective function calculation is:\n```python\ndef evaluate_sequence(seq: list[float]) -> float:\n    n = len(seq)\n    s = np.sum(seq)\n    if s < 0.01 or n == 0:\n        return np.inf\n    conv = np.convolve(seq, seq, mode=\"full\")\n    max_val = np.max(conv)\n    return 2.0 * n * max_val / s**2\n```\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. If a parent sequence is provided, use it as a base candidate\n2. But fallback to uniform [1]*n parent if not.\n3. Define block start/stop indices and pulse widths\n   - Number of distinct pulses: `n_pulses`\n   - Pulse heights: `pulse_heights`\n   - Pulse centers: `pulse_centers`\n\n4. Two-primary generational operators:\n   a. Block crossover: For two candidate solutions, rearrange the pulse components (with greedy validation to avoid overlap over n)\n   b. Local optimization: Use iterative refinement based on the gradient of the objective function (simplified version due to potential numerical issues)\n\n5. Three-secondary strategies:\n   i. Multi-start search: Initialize several candidates of varying pulse counts\n   ii. Downhill simplex (Nelder-Mead): Reimplemented manually for sequences to account for evaluation cost\n   iii. Gradient-based progression: For sufficiently large n, use grid-based aggressive improvement\n\n6. Phase-wise operation:\n   Phase1: `pulse=1` to `pulse=10`\n   Phase2: `pulse=5` to `pulse=25`\n   Phase3: `pulse=10` to `pulse=45`\n   Each phase includes rejuvenation steps to prevent local maxima\n\n7. Fitness calculation per candidate:\n   - Evaluate only those candidates that pass residue and normalization checks\n   - Avoid the infinite\u0437\u043e detector\n   - Only consider candidates with positive or zero values\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Primary: Variable pulse count from 3 to 15\n- Secondary: Adaptive scaling based on binding constraints\n- Evolutionary parameters: \n   - Mutation rate: 0.5% per block\n   - Crossover frequency: Occurs when fit replacement rate is \u226425%\n   - Early termination: If improvement <0.2%, proceed but reduce pulse count variation\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Global time budget: 1500 seconds\n- Per-candidate evaluations: Ricar 5\n- The evolution must not become stuck: respawn time is set to 70% of time spent in pulse count optimization\n- Duplicate sequence evaluations: Only performed when improvement >2%\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Maintenance: Keep the best valid candidate from each generation\n- Normalization: Always renormalize after mutation steps\n- Critical sequence definition: \n   sequence = [float(x) for x in a_valid_sequence]\n   final = evaluate_sequence(sequence)\n   return final is better_score\"\n\n Special handling: sequences with >500 elements require block re-weighting with squared-normalization",
